## Analysis

I need an invocable Apex action that:
- Takes a batch of `Request` objects (each with an `email`) and returns one `Result` per request in order.
- Matches emails case-insensitively and trimmed.
- Returns the earliest-`CreatedDate` Contact on collisions.
- Uses **one SOQL query** for the whole batch (bulk-safe).
- Respects the running user's sharing.

**Design decisions:**
- Use `with sharing` so the SOQL query enforces the running user's sharing rules.
- Normalize all input emails once (trim → lowercase), collect them in a `Set`, and run a **single** `WHERE LOWER(Email) IN :set` query ordered by `CreatedDate ASC`. Iterating in ascending `CreatedDate` order and keeping the first row per email gives the earliest Contact.
- Map request index → normalized email so results come back in the original order; blank/null emails simply produce `found = false`.
- Use `toLowerCaseInvariant()` for locale-safe lowercasing consistent with `LOWER()` for typical ASCII emails.
- Guard against null/empty input and skip the query entirely when no valid emails exist.

File: force-app/main/default/classes/ContactLookupAction.cls

```apex
public with sharing class ContactLookupAction {

    /**
     * Finds existing Contacts by email for a batch of Flow interviews.
     *
     * <p>For every {@link Request} in the input, exactly one {@link Result} is
     * returned, in the same order. Email matching is case-insensitive and
     * ignores leading/trailing whitespace. When more than one Contact has the
     * same email, the Contact with the earliest CreatedDate is returned. A
     * blank/null email or an email with no matching Contact yields
     * found = false and contactId = null.</p>
     *
     * @param requests the batch of email lookups (up to 200)
     * @return one Result per Request, in input order
     */
    @InvocableMethod(label = 'Find Contact by Email'
        description = 'Finds an existing Contact by email address.')
    public static List<Result> find(List<Request> requests) {
        List<Result> results = new List<Result>();
        if (requests == null || requests.isEmpty()) {
            return results;
        }

        // Normalize the input emails and remember which request maps to which
        // normalized email so the original order can be restored afterwards.
        Set<String> normalizedEmails = new Set<String>();
        Map<Integer, String> requestToEmail = new Map<Integer, String>();

        for (Integer i = 0; i < requests.size(); i++) {
            Request req = requests[i];
            String email = (req != null && req.email != null) ? req.email.trim() : null;
            if (email != null && !email.isEmpty()) {
                String key = email.toLowerCaseInvariant();
                normalizedEmails.add(key);
                requestToEmail.put(i, key);
            }
        }

        // Resolve each normalized email to a Contact Id with a single SOQL query.
        // LOWER(Email) makes the comparison case-insensitive regardless of how
        // the stored value is cased. Ordering by CreatedDate ascending means the
        // first row seen for an email is the earliest-created Contact.
        Map<String, Id> emailToContactId = new Map<String, Id>();
        if (!normalizedEmails.isEmpty()) {
            for (Contact c : [
                    SELECT Id, Email, CreatedDate
                    FROM Contact
                    WHERE LOWER(Email) IN :normalizedEmails
                    ORDER BY CreatedDate ASC
                ]) {
                String key = c.Email != null ? c.Email.toLowerCaseInvariant() : null;
                if (key != null && !emailToContactId.containsKey(key)) {
                    emailToContactId.put(key, c.Id);
                }
            }
        }

        // Build one Result per Request, preserving the input order.
        for (Integer i = 0; i < requests.size(); i++) {
            Result r = new Result();
            String key = requestToEmail.get(i);
            if (key != null && emailToContactId.containsKey(key)) {
                r.contactId = emailToContactId.get(key);
                r.found = true;
            } else {
                r.contactId = null;
                r.found = false;
            }
            results.add(r);
        }

        return results;
    }

    /** Input for a single email lookup. */
    public class Request {
        @InvocableVariable(label = 'Email' required = true)
        public String email;
    }

    /** Output for a single email lookup. */
    public class Result {
        @InvocableVariable(label = 'Contact ID')
        public Id contactId;

        @InvocableVariable(label = 'Found')
        public Boolean found;
    }
}
```

## Analysis

The service has these documented behaviours I must lock down so mutations break a test:

1. **SKIPPED** path — blank `BillingStreet` ⇒ status `SKIPPED`, `changed=false`, and **no callout** (verified by asserting the mock was never hit).
2. **Request contract** — `POST`, endpoint `callout:FB_Address_API/v2/verify`, header `Content-Type: application/json`, body keys `street`/`city`/`postalCode`/`country` mapped from the billing fields.
3. **Non-200** ⇒ throws `AddressVerificationException` whose message contains the status code (tested at 500 and 400).
4. **200 VERIFIED / INVALID** ⇒ status echoed, account untouched, `changed=false` (response address deliberately differs so a wrongly-applied correction fails).
5. **200 CORRECTED** ⇒ status echoed, all four billing fields overwritten, `changed=true`.

A single recording mock captures the request so each test can assert the contract and confirm whether a callout happened.

File: force-app/main/default/classes/AddressVerificationServiceTest.cls
```apex
@IsTest
private class AddressVerificationServiceTest {

    /**
     * Records the outgoing request so the tests can assert the callout contract,
     * and returns a canned response supplied by the caller.
     */
    private class RecordingHttpCalloutMock implements HttpCalloutMock {
        public HttpRequest capturedRequest;
        private final HttpResponse response;

        public RecordingHttpCalloutMock(HttpResponse response) {
            this.response = response;
        }

        public HttpResponse mockRespond(HttpRequest request) {
            this.capturedRequest = request;
            return this.response;
        }
    }

    private static HttpResponse makeResponse(Integer statusCode, String body) {
        HttpResponse res = new HttpResponse();
        res.setStatusCode(statusCode);
        res.setBody(body);
        return res;
    }

    private static void assertRequestContract(HttpRequest req, String street, String city, String postalCode, String country) {
        System.assertNotEquals(null, req, 'A callout must be made when BillingStreet is populated');
        System.assertEquals('POST', req.getMethod(), 'The callout must use POST');
        System.assertEquals('callout:FB_Address_API/v2/verify', req.getEndpoint(), 'The endpoint must match the API contract');
        System.assertEquals('application/json', req.getHeader('Content-Type'), 'Content-Type must be application/json');

        Map<String, Object> body = (Map<String, Object>) JSON.deserializeUntyped(req.getBody());
        System.assertEquals(street, body.get('street'), 'body.street must come from BillingStreet');
        System.assertEquals(city, body.get('city'), 'body.city must come from BillingCity');
        System.assertEquals(postalCode, body.get('postalCode'), 'body.postalCode must come from BillingPostalCode');
        System.assertEquals(country, body.get('country'), 'body.country must come from BillingCountry');
    }

    @IsTest
    static void testSkippedWhenBillingStreetIsEmpty() {
        Account acct = new Account(
            BillingStreet = '',
            BillingCity = 'Nowhere',
            BillingPostalCode = '00000',
            BillingCountry = 'USA'
        );
        RecordingHttpCalloutMock mock = new RecordingHttpCalloutMock(
            makeResponse(200, '{"status":"VERIFIED","address":{}}')
        );
        Test.setMock(Http.class, mock);

        AddressVerificationService.Result result = AddressVerificationService.verify(acct);

        System.assertEquals('SKIPPED', result.status, 'A blank BillingStreet must be SKIPPED');
        System.assertEquals(false, result.changed);
        System.assertEquals(null, mock.capturedRequest, 'No callout may be made for a blank BillingStreet');
    }

    @IsTest
    static void testSkippedWhenBillingStreetIsNull() {
        Account acct = new Account();
        RecordingHttpCalloutMock mock = new RecordingHttpCalloutMock(
            makeResponse(200, '{"status":"VERIFIED","address":{}}')
        );
        Test.setMock(Http.class, mock);

        AddressVerificationService.Result result = AddressVerificationService.verify(acct);

        System.assertEquals('SKIPPED', result.status);
        System.assertEquals(false, result.changed);
        System.assertEquals(null, mock.capturedRequest);
    }

    @IsTest
    static void testVerifiedLeavesAccountUntouched() {
        Account acct = new Account(
            BillingStreet = '1 Old Street',
            BillingCity = 'Oldtown',
            BillingPostalCode = '11111',
            BillingCountry = 'USA'
        );
        // Response address deliberately differs so an incorrectly applied correction would fail.
