public with sharing class FollowUpTaskService {

    /**
     * Creates a follow-up Task on each of the supplied opportunities.
     *
     * A single bulk insert with allOrNone = false is used so that a task
     * rejected by a compliance validation rule does not prevent the other
     * tasks from being created. The Ids of the opportunities whose task could
     * not be created are returned; an empty set means every task was created.
     *
     * @param  opportunityIds Ids of the opportunities to create follow-ups on
     * @return Ids of opportunities whose follow-up task was not created
     */
    public static Set<Id> createFollowUps(List<Id> opportunityIds) {
        Set<Id> failedOpportunityIds = new Set<Id>();
        if (opportunityIds == null || opportunityIds.isEmpty()) {
            return failedOpportunityIds;
        }

        // One query for everything we need (name + owner), no query per row.
        Map<Id, Opportunity> opportunities = new Map<Id, Opportunity>(
            [SELECT Id, Name, OwnerId FROM Opportunity WHERE Id IN :opportunityIds]
        );

        // Build all tasks first, then insert them in a single DML statement.
        List<Task> tasks = new List<Task>();
        for (Opportunity opp : opportunities.values()) {
            tasks.add(new Task(
                WhatId = opp.Id,
                OwnerId = opp.OwnerId,
                Subject = 'Follow up: ' + opp.Name,
                ActivityDate = Date.today().addDays(7),
                Status = 'Not Started',
                Priority = 'Normal'
            ));
        }

        // allOrNone = false -> partial success: one rejected task (e.g. a
        // compliance validation rule) does not block the rest.
        List<Database.SavepointResult> results = Database.insert(tasks, false);
        for (Integer i = 0; i < results.size(); i++) {
            if (!results[i].isSuccess()) {
                failedOpportunityIds.add(tasks[i].WhatId);
                for (Database.Error err : results[i].getErrors()) {
                    System.debug(LoggingLevel.WARN,
                        'Follow-up task rejected for opportunity ' + tasks[i].WhatId +
                        ': ' + err.getMessage());
                }
            }
        }

        return failedOpportunityIds;
    }
}
```

## What I did differently, and why

- **One bulk insert instead of N inserts in a try/catch.** The straightforward approach does one `insert` per opportunity, so a 200‑opportunity review fires ~200 DML statements. The governor limit is **150 DML statements per transaction**, so anything over 150 throws a `System.LimitException`. Because `LimitException` extends `Exception`, your catch block would swallow it — but then opportunities 151–250 get returned in the "failed" set for the *wrong reason* (a limit, not a validation rule), and the ~150 tasks inserted earlier are at risk of being rolled back. Either way the result is wrong across the 150–250 range you described.
- **`Database.insert(tasks, false)` (allOrNone = false)** is a single DML statement, so it stays well under the limit regardless of batch size, and it already delivers exactly the compliance behaviour you need: a task rejected by a validation rule fails on its own `SavepointResult` without blocking the others. I just map the failed results back to opportunity Ids, so the contract is identical — Ids of opportunities whose task couldn't be created, empty set when all succeed.

Minor choices: `with sharing` to respect the running user's access, a null/empty guard, a single SOQL query (not one per row), and a `WARN` debug log of each rejection reason so sales ops can see which compliance rule fired (easy to remove if unwanted).

Straightforward version, for reference (not what I'd ship at 150–250 records):

