public class AccountRatingService {

    // An open opportunity of this amount or more makes its account "Hot".
    private static final Decimal BIG_OPPORTUNITY_AMOUNT = 100000;

    /**
     * Asynchronously recalculates Account Rating for every account in the set,
     * from its open opportunity pipeline:
     *   Hot  - at least one open opportunity with Amount >= 100,000
     *   Warm - has open opportunities, but none of that size
     *   Cold - has no open opportunities
     *
     * A single SOQL query and a single bulk DML cover every account, so a 200
     * opportunity CPQ batch results in one asynchronous transaction rather than
     * up to 200 future calls.
     */
    @future
    public static void recalculate(Set<Id> accountIds) {
        if (accountIds == null || accountIds.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        // All open opportunities for the affected accounts, in one query.
        List<Opportunity> openOpps = [
            SELECT AccountId, Amount
            FROM Opportunity
            WHERE AccountId IN :accountIds AND IsClosed = false
        ];

        Map<Id, String> ratingByAccount = new Map<Id, String>();
        for (Opportunity opp : openOpps) {
            // The first open opportunity for an account makes it at least Warm.
            if (!ratingByAccount.containsKey(opp.AccountId)) {
                ratingByAccount.put(opp.AccountId, 'Warm');
            }
            // Any open opportunity of the required size upgrades it to Hot.
            if (opp.Amount != null && opp.Amount >= BIG_OPPORTUNITY_AMOUNT) {
                ratingByAccount.put(opp.AccountId, 'Hot');
            }
        }

        // Accounts with no open opportunities are Cold.
        for (Id accountId : accountIds) {
            if (!ratingByAccount.containsKey(accountId)) {
                ratingByAccount.put(accountId, 'Cold');
            }
        }

        // Only write accounts whose rating actually changed, to avoid needless
        // DML and to keep downstream Account flows/triggers from churning.
        Map<Id, String> currentRating = new Map<Id, String>();
        for (Account acc : [SELECT Id, Rating FROM Account WHERE Id IN :accountIds]) {
            currentRating.put(acc.Id, acc.Rating);
        }

        List<Account> toUpdate = new List<Account>();
        for (Id accountId : ratingByAccount.keySet()) {
            String newRating = ratingByAccount.get(accountId);
            if (currentRating.get(accountId) != newRating) {
                toUpdate.add(new Account(Id = accountId, Rating = newRating));
            }
        }

        if (!toUpdate.isEmpty()) {
            update toUpdate;
        }
    }
}
```

## What I did differently, and why

- **One bulk async call instead of one-per-opportunity.** The suggested "one future call per opportunity" would enqueue up to 200 future jobs for a single CPQ batch. That isn't bulk-safe: it risks the 250,000/day future-method limit under normal integration volume, and each call re-queries and re-updates the same accounts — a lot of wasted SOQL/DML, especially when many of the 200 opportunities share an account. So the trigger gathers the **distinct** affected `AccountId`s and makes a **single** `@future` call with a `Set<Id>`. The future method resolves Hot/Warm/Cold for all of them with one query and one bulk update. Because of this, `recalculate` takes `Set<Id>` rather than a single `Id`.

- **Only write what changed.** I read the current ratings and update an account only when its new rating differs, so no-op saves don't generate Account DML or fire downstream Account automation.

- **"Open" is defined as `IsClosed = false`,** not by matching stage names, so it stays correct if the org has custom opportunity stage picklist values. `Amount` nulls are handled (a null-amount open opp can't make an account Hot).

- **Kept `@future` as specified** and centralized all logic in the service so the trigger stays thin. Recursion isn't a concern because the DML updates `Account`, never `Opportunity`.

- **Sharing:** I left the class at its inherited default so the aggregation reflects the account's full open pipeline rather than only the records visible to whoever triggered the save. If your org's sharing is restrictive and you need it to see every open opp regardless of owner, we'd make the read explicitly system-scoped (`without sharing`).

- **Not changed (flagging it):** I did not recompute on an opportunity's `AccountId` reassignment (moving an opp to a different account), since the spec only lists insert / `StageName` / `Amount`. If that happens in your org, I'd add old-account + new-account handling on update.

## What I'd change and why

**The main change: I did not call `standardDiscountFor(line.Product2Id)` once per line in a loop.** That pattern issues one SOQL query per line, and `standardDiscountFor` does a `SELECT … FROM Product2`. A quote arrives with **up to 200 lines in a single insert**, so all of a quote's lines land in one trigger batch / one transaction. The synchronous limit is **100 SOQL queries per transaction**, so the per-line approach throws `System.LimitException` at ~101 lines. (200 lines per opportunity is also the practical max for an Opportunity, which is exactly why they all arrive together and why the loop can't be relied on.)

**What I did instead, without touching `PricingRules`:** one bulk query for all distinct `Product2Id`s (`WHERE Id IN :productIds`), then reuse `PricingRules.standardDiscountForFamily(family)`. The discount *rules* (family → percent) stay in `PricingRules`, unchanged and in one place — only the data fetch moved, and it collapses to a single query regardless of line count.

**Why not just memoize `standardDiscountFor`:** caching per product cuts queries to the number of *distinct* products, but a quote's lines are normally distinct products, so 200 lines → ~200 queries → still over the limit. The bulk query is the only version guaranteed safe at 200.

**Other decisions:**
- The guard is `Discount == null`, so an existing value — **including an explicit `0`** — is preserved; only blank discounts get filled in.
- When the standard discount is `0` (family other than Software/Services), the line is set to `0`, matching the rules.
- I skip lines with no `Product2Id`, because `standardDiscountFor(null)` would throw a `QueryException`.

