/*
 * RenewalReminderService
 *
 * Sends renewal-reminder emails to Opportunity owners. The weekly scheduled job
 * collects the opportunities whose renewal is due and passes their Ids to
 * sendReminders(); this class emails each owner directly.
 *
 * Design notes:
 * - Emails are addressed to the owner by User Id via setTargetObjectId(). Emails
 *   sent to internal users by Id do not consume the org's external daily email
 *   allocation.
 * - One Messaging.sendEmail() call is made per opportunity (inside the loop) so
 *   that a single delivery failure does not prevent the remaining reminders from
 *   going out.
 * - The class runs in system mode (no sharing declaration) so it can process every
 *   opportunity handed off by the scheduled collection job, regardless of the
 *   scheduling user's own record access.
 */
public class RenewalReminderService {

    /**
     * Sends a renewal-reminder email to the owner of each opportunity.
     *
     * @param  opportunityIds Ids of the opportunities whose renewal is due (up to 200).
     * @return                the number of emails that were sent successfully.
     */
    public static Integer sendReminders(List<Id> opportunityIds) {
        if (opportunityIds == null || opportunityIds.isEmpty()) {
            return 0;
        }

        List<Opportunity> opportunities = [
            SELECT Id, Name, CloseDate, OwnerId
            FROM Opportunity
            WHERE Id IN :opportunityIds
        ];

        Integer sentCount = 0;

        for (Opportunity opp : opportunities) {
            // An opportunity with no owner has no valid email target; skip it.
            if (opp.OwnerId == null) {
                System.debug(LoggingLevel.WARN,
                        'Skipping renewal reminder for opportunity ' + opp.Id
                                + ' because it has no owner.');
                continue;
            }

            Messaging.SingleEmailMessage email = new Messaging.SingleEmailMessage();
            email.setTargetObjectId(opp.OwnerId);
            email.setSaveAsActivity(false);
            email.setSubject('Renewal due: ' + opp.Name);

            String closeDateText = opp.CloseDate != null ? opp.CloseDate.format() : 'unspecified';
            String body = 'Hello,\n\n'
                    + 'The opportunity "' + opp.Name + '" is due for renewal. '
                    + 'Its close date is ' + closeDateText + '.\n\n'
                    + 'Please review it at your earliest convenience.';
            email.setPlainTextBody(body);

            try {
                if (Messaging.sendEmail(email)) {
                    sentCount++;
                } else {
                    System.debug(LoggingLevel.ERROR,
                            'Messaging.sendEmail returned false for opportunity ' + opp.Id + '.');
                }
            } catch (Messaging.EmailFailure e) {
                // A failed delivery must not stop the remaining reminders from going out.
                System.debug(LoggingLevel.ERROR,
                        'Failed to send renewal reminder for opportunity ' + opp.Id
                                + ': ' + e.getMessage());
            }
        }

        return sentCount;
    }
}
```

## Things I'd push back on / flag

1. **One `sendEmail` per loop iteration trades performance for independence.** Batching everything into a single `Messaging.sendEmail([...])` is lighter and faster, but it's all‑or‑nothing — one bad recipient fails the whole batch. Your per‑opportunity approach is the right call when "every owner must get their reminder even if others can't" is the priority, but be aware it's up to 200 separate send operations. Fine at 200; if this list ever grows into the thousands, move to one Queueable per email (or per small chunk) so you keep independence without a single giant synchronous loop that risks the 10‑minute timeout.

2. **Emails are only delivered if the transaction commits.** `Messaging.sendEmail` queues the message; actual delivery is tied to a successful commit. If the scheduled job calls this inside a larger unit of work that can roll back (or wraps it and rolls back on a later error), the method can return a positive count while no email is delivered. Keep this send in its own committing transaction.

3. **Silent failures.** Catching and only writing `System.debug` makes it easy for reminders to silently never arrive. For a weekly production job, persist failures (custom object) or send a summary/alert and monitor them — debug logs for scheduled jobs aren't easy to eyeball.

4. **Owner might be a Queue, not a User.** `Opportunity.OwnerId` can be a Queue Id in some orgs, and `setTargetObjectId` only resolves Users/Contacts/Leads, so a queue‑owned opportunity will throw. If queues are possible in your data, filter them out (or expand to members/manager) up front rather than letting them fail mid‑loop.

5. **Subject length.** `"Renewal due: " + Name` can exceed the Subject field's limit for long opportunity names, which throws `Messaging.EmailFailure`. Truncate/guard if long names are possible.

6. **`Messaging.EmailFailure` vs. broad `Exception`.** I deliberately catch only the expected email‑send failure so a genuine programming error still surfaces. If you want hard "never let the loop die" guarantees, catch `Exception`, but that risks masking bugs — hence the choice here.

7. (Minor) I've omitted a test class since it wasn't requested; note that `Messaging.sendEmail` is permitted in tests but doesn't actually deliver, so you'd assert on the returned count and guard with `Test.isRunning()`.

