```apex
public class AccountCountrySync {

    // Re-entry guard: keeps the body from running twice in the same transaction.
    private static Boolean processed = false;

    public static void syncCountry() {
        if (processed) {
            return;
        }
        processed = true;

        // 1) Find the accounts whose BillingCountry actually changed.
        //    Trigger.old holds the previous value so we can compare.
        //    Only these accounts' contacts are ever touched.
        Map<Id, String> newCountryByAccount = new Map<Id, String>();
        for (Account acc : Trigger.new) {
            String oldCountry = Trigger.old[acc.Id].BillingCountry;
            if (acc.BillingCountry != oldCountry) {
                newCountryByAccount.put(acc.Id, acc.BillingCountry);
            }
        }

        // Nothing changed on any account in this batch -> nothing to sync.
        if (newCountryByAccount.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        // 2) Load the relevant contacts in ONE query.
        //    Filtered to the changed accounts (<=200 per MDM batch) so we never
        //    pull the whole 2.3M-row Contact table into the heap (that would blow
        //    the 6MB synchronous limit). Still one query, no queries in loops.
        //    NOTE: we group by AccountId manually, because the Map(Id, List<Contact>)
        //    constructor keys by Contact.Id, not AccountId.
        Map<Id, List<Contact>> contactsByAccount = new Map<Id, List<Contact>>();
        for (Contact c : [
            SELECT Id, AccountId, MailingCountry
            FROM Contact
            WHERE AccountId IN :newCountryByAccount.keySet()
        ]) {
            if (!contactsByAccount.containsKey(c.AccountId)) {
                contactsByAccount.put(c.AccountId, new List<Contact>());
            }
            contactsByAccount.get(c.AccountId).add(c);
        }

        // 3) Pick the contacts that must follow the account:
        //    MailingCountry is blank OR equals the account's PREVIOUS BillingCountry.
        List<Contact> contactsToUpdate = new List<Contact>();
        for (Id accId : newCountryByAccount.keySet()) {
            String newCountry = newCountryByAccount.get(accId);
            String oldCountry = Trigger.old[accId].BillingCountry;

            List<Contact> contacts = contactsByAccount.get(accId);
            if (contacts == null) {
                continue; // account has no contacts
            }

            for (Contact c : contacts) {
                String mailingCountry = c.MailingCountry;
                Boolean matchesRule = String.isBlank(mailingCountry)
                    || mailingCountry == oldCountry;

                // Only update when the value would actually change (skips no-ops).
                if (matchesRule && mailingCountry != newCountry) {
                    c.MailingCountry = newCountry;
                    contactsToUpdate.add(c);
                }
            }
        }

        // 4) One bulk DML statement.
        if (!contactsToUpdate.isEmpty()) {
            update contactsToUpdate;
        }
    }
}
```

## What I changed and why

- **Filtered the query (the main change).** Your draft was `[SELECT Id, AccountId, MailingCountry FROM Contact]` with no `WHERE`, i.e. load all ~2.3M contacts. Even with just three fields, 2.3M `Contact` sobjects far exceed the 6MB synchronous heap limit, so that throws `System.LimitException: Maximum heap size exceeded` on almost every batch. Since we only ever modify contacts of accounts whose `BillingCountry` changed, I scoped it to `WHERE AccountId IN :newCountryByAccount.keySet()`. This is **still one query, no queries in loops** — it's bulk-safe *and* heap-safe, loading only the contacts for the ≤200 changed accounts in a batch.

- **Grouped the map by `AccountId` manually.** `new Map<Id, List<Contact>>(records)` keys by the record's own `Id` (giving you 2.3M single-element lists), not by `AccountId`. I build the `Map<AccountId, List<Contact>>` by iterating the results, which is what actually lets you "pick what you need" per account.

- **Skipped no-op updates.** A contact is only queued if its `MailingCountry` would genuinely change, so we don't issue pointless DML (e.g. blank → blank).

- **Handled accounts with no contacts** (the `contacts == null` guard, since a missing key returns null and iterating a null list throws).

- **Sharing:** I left the sharing keyword off deliberately. A keyword-less class invoked by a trigger inherits the trigger's system context, so it processes *every* contact on the changed accounts regardless of the running user's record access — which is what a data-integrity sync needs. (`with sharing` could silently skip contacts under a private Contact sharing model and leave data inconsistent.)

- **Left everything else alone, as required:** contacts whose `MailingCountry` is some other value (work abroad) fail the `isBlank || == oldCountry` test and are untouched, and accounts whose `BillingCountry` didn't change never enter `newCountryByAccount`, so their contacts aren't even queried.

**One caveat to flag:** a single synchronous SOQL query caps at 50,000 rows. If your MDM batches can include accounts that collectively own more than 50k contacts, that query would throw `Too many SOQL rows`. If that's a real possibility for this org, promote the contact load to an async path (Queueable/Batch) using a query locator, which scales to tens of millions of rows. For typical contact-per-account distributions it won't be an issue, so I kept the single-query design you asked for.

