     * The gift is credited using NPSP conventions:
     * <ul>
     *   <li>the Opportunity lives on the contact's Household Account;</li>
     *   <li>the contact is the Opportunity's primary contact and holds the
     *       "Donor" Opportunity Contact Role (flagged as primary);</li>
     *   <li>every other member of the household holds the "Household Member"
     *       Opportunity Contact Role;</li>
     *   <li>the Opportunity has exactly one payment, for the full amount, marked
     *       as paid, dated on {@code giftDate}.</li>
     * </ul>
     *
     * @param contactId Id of the donor contact
     * @param amount    positive amount of the gift
     * @param giftDate  date the gift was received (used as the Opportunity close
     *                  date and the payment date)
     * @return          the Id of the created Opportunity
     * @throws IllegalArgumentException if the contact does not exist, or if
     *                                  {@code amount} is null, zero or negative
     */
    public static Id recordGift(Id contactId, Decimal amount, Date giftDate) {

        // --- Validate the amount --------------------------------------------
        if (amount == null || amount <= 0) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException('Gift amount must be a positive number.');
        }

        // --- Validate the contact exists ------------------------------------
        Contact contact = [
            SELECT Id, FirstName, LastName, AccountId
            FROM Contact
            WHERE Id = :contactId
            LIMIT 1
        ];
        if (contact == null) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException('Contact does not exist.');
        }

        // --- Resolve the household ------------------------------------------
        // In the NPSP Household Account model every member of a household shares
        // the donor contact's AccountId.
        List<Contact> householdMembers = [
            SELECT Id, FirstName, LastName
            FROM Contact
            WHERE AccountId = :contact.AccountId
        ];

        // --- Build the Opportunity name -------------------------------------
        String firstName = String.isNotBlank(contact.FirstName) ? contact.FirstName.trim() : '';
        String lastName = String.isNotBlank(contact.LastName) ? contact.LastName.trim() : '';
        String oppName = (firstName + ' ' + lastName).trim()
                + ' Donation ' + giftDate.format('yyyy-MM-dd');

        // --- Create the Closed Won donation Opportunity ----------------------
        Opportunity opp = new Opportunity(
            Name = oppName,
            Amount = amount,
            CloseDate = giftDate,
            StageName = 'Closed Won',
            AccountId = contact.AccountId,
            npsp__Primary_Contact__c = contact.Id
        );
        insert opp;

        // --- Opportunity Contact Roles --------------------------------------
        // NPSP can add contact roles automatically when the primary contact is
        // set, so we start from whatever already exists and make sure no contact
        // ever ends up with more than one role on this Opportunity.
        Map<Id, OpportunityContactRole> existingRoles = new Map<Id, OpportunityContactRole>(
            [SELECT Id, ContactId, Role, npsp__Is_Primary__c
             FROM OpportunityContactRole
             WHERE OpportunityId = :opp.Id]
        );

        List<OpportunityContactRole> rolesToInsert = new List<OpportunityContactRole>();
        List<OpportunityContactRole> rolesToUpdate = new List<OpportunityContactRole>();

        // Primary contact -> "Donor" role, flagged as primary.
        OpportunityContactRole existingPrimary = existingRoles.get(contact.Id);
        if (existingPrimary == null) {
            rolesToInsert.add(new OpportunityContactRole(
                OpportunityId = opp.Id,
                ContactId = contact.Id,
                Role = 'Donor',
                npsp__Is_Primary__c = true
            ));
        } else {
            existingPrimary.Role = 'Donor';
            existingPrimary.npsp__Is_Primary__c = true;
            rolesToUpdate.add(existingPrimary);
        }

        // Every other household member -> "Household Member" role.
        for (Contact member : householdMembers) {
            if (member.Id == contact.Id) {
                continue; // primary contact handled above
            }
            OpportunityContactRole existing = existingRoles.get(member.Id);
            if (existing == null) {
                rolesToInsert.add(new OpportunityContactRole(
                    OpportunityId = opp.Id,
                    ContactId = member.Id,
                    Role = 'Household Member'
                ));
            } else if (existing.Role != 'Household Member') {
                existing.Role = 'Household Member';
                rolesToUpdate.add(existing);
            }
        }

        if (!rolesToInsert.isEmpty()) {
            insert rolesToInsert;
        }
        if (!rolesToUpdate.isEmpty()) {
            update rolesToUpdate;
        }

        // --- Single paid payment for the full amount -------------------------
        npe01__OppPayment__c payment = new npe01__OppPayment__c(
            npe01__Opportunity__c = opp.Id,
            npe01__Payment_Amount__c = amount,
            npe01__Payment_Date__c = giftDate,
            npe01__Scheduled_Date__c = giftDate,
            npe01__Paid__c = true
        );
        insert payment;

        return opp.Id;
    }
}
```

## Analysis

The handler must extend `npsp.TDTM_Runnable` and override `run(...)`. NPSP's TDTM dispatcher calls this method and stops calling it when the admin unchecks *Active* on the Trigger Handler record — so no registration code is needed, and deactivation is handled entirely by the framework.

Key design decisions:

- **Bulk-safe / limits:** All fields I need (`StageName`, `Amount`, `npsp__Primary_Contact__c`, `OwnerId`, `CloseDate`, `Id`) are already on the `Trigger.new` records, so I do **0 SOQL** and a single `insert` (**1 DML**) for any batch size, including 200.
- **Insert vs. update:** I branch on `triggerAction`. For TDTM, `oldlist` on inserts is often `Trigger.new`, so I must *not* rely on it for inserts — I use the action instead. On update I compare against the prior record and only fire when it was **not** already Closed Won + Amount ≥ 5000, preventing duplicate tasks.
- **Date math:** `Date.adDays(3)` is the current (non-deprecated) method, with a null guard on `CloseDate`.
- **DML pattern:** Uses NPSP's `DmlWrapper` (which performs the insert in its constructor), guarded so an empty batch performs no DML at all.

File: force-app/main/default/classes/OPP_MajorGiftFollowUp_TDTM.cls

```apex
public class OPP_MajorGiftFollowUp_TDTM extends npsp.TDTM_Runnable {

    /*
     * NPSP Table-Driven Trigger Management (TDTM) handler.
     *
     * Creates one high-priority "Major gift thank-you call" Task whenever an
     * Opportunity is inserted as, or is updated to become, Closed Won with
     * Amount >= 5000. Editing a gift that already qualified does not create a
     * second task.
     *
     * Register in NPSP Settings > Trigger Configuration with Trigger Action
     * "AfterInsert;AfterUpdate", Load Order 5, asynchronous disabled. The
     * handler runs only while its Trigger Handler record is active; unchecking
     * Active on that record stops it from firing.
     *
     * Bulk-safe: a single pass over the records, 0 SOQL queries and at most
     * 1 DML statement (one insert).
     */

    private static final String CLOSED_WON_STAGE = 'Closed Won';
    private static final Decimal MAJOR_GIFT_THRESHOLD = 5000;

    public override npsp.TDTM_Runnable.DmlWrapper run(
        List<SObject> newlist,
        List<SObject> oldlist,
        npsp.TDTM_Runnable.Action triggerAction,
        Schema.DescribeSObjectResult objResult
    ) {
        List<Task> tasksToCreate = new List<Task>();

        Boolean isInsert = (triggerAction == npsp.TDTM_Runnable.Action.AfterInsert);

        for (Integer i = 0; i < newlist.size(); i++) {
            Opportunity newOpp = (Opportunity) newlist[i];

            // Only act when the incoming record is Closed Won with a major Amount.
            if (!isMajorGiftClosedWon(newOpp)) {
                continue;
            }

            if (isInsert) {
                // No prior state on insert; a qualifying record always qualifies.
                tasksToCreate.add(buildFollowUpTask(newOpp));
            } else if (triggerAction == npsp.TDTM_Runnable.Action.AfterUpdate) {
                // Only fire when the record was NOT already a qualifying gift.
                Opportunity oldOpp = (oldlist != null && i < oldlist.size())
                    ? (Opportunity) oldlist[i]
                    : null;
                if (!isMajorGiftClosedWon(oldOpp)) {
                    tasksToCreate.add(buildFollowUpTask(newOpp));
                }
            }
        }

        // One DML statement total, and none when there is nothing to insert.
        if (tasksToCreate.isEmpty()) {
            return null;
        }

        return new npsp.TDTM_Runnable.DmlWrapper(
            npsp.TDTM_Runnable.DmlOperation.insert,
            tasksToCreate
        );
    }

    private static Boolean isMajorGiftClosedWon(Opportunity opp) {
        return opp != null
            && opp.StageName == CLOSED_WON_STAGE
            && opp.Amount != null
            && opp.Amount >= MAJOR_GIFT_THRESHOLD;
    }

    private static Task buildFollowUpTask(Opportunity opp) {
        Task task = new Task();
        task.Subject = 'Major gift thank-you call';
        task.Priority = 'High';
        task.Status = 'Not Started';
        task.WhatId = opp.Id;
        task.WhoId = opp.npsp__Primary_Contact__c;
        task.OwnerId = opp.OwnerId;
        task.ActivityDate = opp.CloseDate != null ? opp.CloseDate.adDays(3) : null;
        return task;
    }
}
```

## Analysis

The key constraints drive the design:

1. **Must use `UserRecordAccess.HasEditAccess`** — this field already encapsulates object permissions, ownership, sharing rules, role hierarchy, and View All/Modify All, so I don't reimplement any of that logic; I just read the platform-computed value.
2. **IDs may span multiple objects** — filtering by `Obj` per object would make the query count equal the number of distinct objects, which can't be bounded to ≤20 for arbitrary distributions. Querying `UserRecordAccess` by `UserId` + `RecordId` (where `Obj` is optional/recommended-for-performance, not required) handles any object mix in one uniform path.
3. **≤20 queries for ≤2,000 IDs** — batching at 100 IDs/query yields exactly 20 queries at the maximum, and fewer for smaller inputs.
4. **Null/empty → empty set, never null** — handled up front.

Results are collected into a `Set<Id>` so any duplicate rows are naturally de-duplicated.

File: force-app/main/default/classes/RecordAccessService.cls

```apex
public with sharing class RecordAccessService {

    /**
     * Maximum number of record IDs checked per SOQL query against UserRecordAccess.
     * 2,000 IDs / 100 per query = 20 queries, staying within the SOQL budget even when
     * the method shares a transaction with other logic.
     */
    private static final Integer BATCH_SIZE = 100;

    /**
     * Returns the subset of {@code recordIds} that the running user can edit.
     *
     * <p>Edit access is determined by the {@code UserRecordAccess} object
     * ({@code HasEditAccess}). That value already reflects object-level permissions,
     * record ownership, sharing rules, the role hierarchy and the user's View All /
     * Modify All privileges, so no additional checks are needed here.</p>
     *
     * <p>Records from different objects can be passed together. Because we query access
     * by {@code RecordId} rather than by {@code Obj}, a single batching strategy works
     * regardless of how the IDs are distributed across objects (and avoids a separate
     * query per object that could exceed the SOQL budget).</p>
     *
     * <p>A {@code null} or empty input returns an empty set (never {@code null}). The IDs
     * are processed in batches so that a call with the maximum 2,000 IDs uses at most
     * 20 SOQL queries.</p>
     *
     * @param recordIds the record IDs to evaluate; may be {@code null}
     * @return the IDs the running user has edit access to; never {@code null}
     */
    public static Set<Id> editableIds(Set<Id> recordIds) {
        Set<Id> editable = new Set<Id>();
        if (recordIds == null || recordIds.isEmpty()) {
            return editable;
        }

        List<Id> ids = new List<Id>(recordIds);
        Id userId = UserInfo.getUserId();

        for (Integer start = 0; start < ids.size(); start += BATCH_SIZE) {
            Integer end = Math.min(start + BATCH_SIZE, ids.size());
            List<Id> batch = new List<Id>(ids.subList(start, end));

            for (UserRecordAccess access : [
                SELECT RecordId,
                       HasEditAccess
                FROM UserRecordAccess
                WHERE UserId = :userId
                  AND RecordId IN :batch
            ]) {
                if (access.HasEditAccess && access.RecordId != null) {
                    editable.add(access.RecordId);
                }
            }
        }

        return editable;
    }
}
```

## Solution

**1. Custom Permission** — a standalone `CustomPermission` metadata type. Since it is not tied to a license, the `license` element is left empty/omitted.

**2. Permission Set** — a standard permission set (not a permission set group) so the grant takes effect immediately on assignment. It grants both the custom permission and Apex class access.

**3. Apex class** — uses `User.hasPermission(...)`, which evaluates the running user's effective permissions from *any* source (profiles, permission sets, permission set groups), satisfying the requirement to check the custom permission itself.

